7592186074613 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074613 #edb/ref 1061 172324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074613 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "242be67e-027e-54a4-9866-c63cb081faa9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074613 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074614 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074614 #edb/ref 1061 172327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074614 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7721ecc8-af47-5723-b955-d68c3dc4eecb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074614 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074615 #edb/ref 1061 172330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074615 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b662cd66-040d-56ae-be80-ddf72c03e975" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074615 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074736 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074736 #edb/ref 1061 39867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074736 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cf5a7b59-9df0-505a-86e0-c7437dc8b262" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074736 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186075016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074737 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074737 #edb/ref 1061 39868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074737 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f29f88a5-25e3-54f3-b724-70c3d82de050" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074737 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074738 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074738 #edb/ref 1061 39869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074738 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ec8f3fd6-e68a-5a08-909a-7ca8734e46ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074738 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074739 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074739 #edb/ref 1061 39871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074739 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6cd88bb7-dfba-55b2-bb7a-0cce2188c27c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074739 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074740 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074740 #edb/ref 1061 39884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074740 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0ff59236-e8f5-575d-bc8b-731fac2b148a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074740 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186075017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074741 #edb/ref 1061 39885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074741 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "111068bf-412d-586d-b167-f6557ad36f93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074741 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074903 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074903 #edb/ref 1061 93403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074903 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "65c45801-df02-5e24-9d1c-d20654b36c65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074903 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186075046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074904 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074904 #edb/ref 1061 93404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074904 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "300cdd9f-b0a5-526a-907d-3323aed6cfc9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074904 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074905 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074905 #edb/ref 1061 93407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074905 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2868f3bf-331f-5d98-b8a2-329c0d7d3ceb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074905 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074906 #edb/ref 1061 93410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074906 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "29859205-5375-5c4e-971d-54f1c05b3f49" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074906 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075016 #edb/ref 1064 "We can use fraction models to multiply unit fractions by whole numbers. \n\nFor example, let's consider the following product: \n\n$$\n\\dfrac 13 \\times 2\n$$\n\nFirst, we represent $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ using a model:\n\n![](images/f0/f08054646558ab944940f1f8f0d8a1077748f9882039e775c6cbdd4d307db102.png)\n\n \n\nMultiplication by a whole number is simply a repeated addition. And, since we need to multiply $\\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{3}$ by ${\\color{blue}2},$ we repeat the fraction model $\\color{blue}2$ times, as shown below.\n\n![](images/31/31fcef6d631262e49fc9c9297fd006f935fd7ee8182b0074d77ae22b626efbbc.png)\n\n \n\nThere are ${\\color{blue}2} \\times {\\color{red}1} = 2$ shaded pieces in total. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2 \\times \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{2}{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075016 #edb/ref 1065 6606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075016 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0baa395e-db83-5229-83f7-f40fd99e8a4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075016 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075017 #edb/ref 1064 "There's another way we can use fraction models to multiply unit fractions by whole numbers.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the multiplication problem below:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10} \\times 7\n$$\n\nWe start by drawing a fraction model to represent $\\dfrac{1}{10}.$\n\n![](images/62/62b50e658ed9bc691a9559548d09f87fbd3b33e007e81b5e78a3235ac4bc3280.png)\n\n \n\nNow, rather than repeating our model $7$ times (which would take a long time), we can instead multiply the number of shaded pieces by $7.$ \n\nOur model has $1$ shaded piece. So, the resulting model must have $1 \\times 7 = 7$ shaded pieces: \n\n![](images/2a/2a7d54b1d7a6a6013ee64b601d9dc0ba31c8a25dae0f5f69abba4b0fed0e8ee2.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10}\\times 7 = \\dfrac{7}{10}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075017 #edb/ref 1065 6608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075017 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5b799a98-0619-525c-b2da-433704f26bd8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075017 #edb/ref 1067 "A Faster Way of Computing a Product Using Models" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075043 #edb/ref 1064 "Remember that a **unit fraction** is a fraction containing $1$ in the numerator. \n\nFor example, the following fractions are unit fractions since they each contain ${\\color{blue}{1}}$ in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{1}}{2}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{blue}{1}}{5}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{blue}{1}}{8}\n$$\n\nHowever, the following fractions are *not* unit fractions since they do *not* contain ${\\color{blue}{1}}$ in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}{2}}{3}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{red}{5}}{8}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{red}{7}}{12}\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we will use models to divide whole numbers by unit fractions. \n\nLet's start with an example. Suppose we divide the pizza below into fifths. How many fifths will there be in total?\n\n![](images/4a/4a73488a784067fbe0a4281ef8de45209e284bcd8675f5476c14a8bdcb357712.png)\n\n \n\nSince each part is $\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5}$ of a whole, we need to split $\\color{red}1$ pizza (whole) into $\\color{blue}5$ equal parts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/9e/9e36c7b607bd6ada0018e574d52c060851837b62025429b6cfb94f98ed378ccd.png)\n\n \n\nSo, we get ${\\color{red}1} \\times {\\color{blue}5} = 5$ parts in total." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075043 #edb/ref 1065 8557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075043 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "577d7682-3ed8-5103-8641-0c02702a9e08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075043 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075044 #edb/ref 1064 "The model below represents $2$ wholes. Let's use it to calculate the value of $2 \\div \\dfrac{1}{5}.$\n\n![](images/bc/bceafee7d9ca5e92e69b5d33d9504f4290526b80059ad62ed26229d02712a278.png)\n\n \n\nTo calculate ${\\color{red}2} \\div \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5}$, we need to divide each of $\\color{red}2$ wholes into $\\color{blue}5$ equal parts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/cf/cf608ce8f5d8e2a47a4bebe21f198d193b10dd8f6e95436986bc954c89c60d19.png)\n\n \n\nWe get ${\\color{red}2} \\times {\\color{blue}5} = 10$ pieces in total. \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2 \\div \\dfrac{1}{5} = 10\\,.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075044 #edb/ref 1065 8561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075044 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2de869cb-775d-5db4-b90d-7df0de218fc0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075044 #edb/ref 1067 "Dividing Whole Numbers by Unit Fractions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075045 #edb/ref 1064 "To divide a unit fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's denominator by the whole number.\n\nFor example, to calculate $\\dfrac{1}{5} \\div {\\color{blue}2},$ we multiply the fraction's denominator by ${\\color{blue}2}\\!:$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{5} \\div {\\color{blue}2}  = \\dfrac{1}{5 \\times {\\color{blue}2}} = \\dfrac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nNotice that we get the same answer using a fraction model, and the process is similar.\n\n![](images/3f/3f1f0dddd115c0d820de4d79624bd87492e215a6c2de43071fda73a4a0684d77.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075045 #edb/ref 1065 8562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075045 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fcf4ea8b-7765-55e8-b5c1-a10899bee9f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075045 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075046 #edb/ref 1064 "To divide a whole number by a unit fraction, we can multiply the whole number by the fraction's denominator.\n\nSo, to divide $2 \\div \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5},$ we multiply the $2$ by ${\\color{blue}5}\\!:$\n\n$$\n2 \\div \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5} = 2 \\times {\\color{blue}5} = 10\n$$\n\nAnd that's the answer! Notice that this method does the same job as the fraction model method we used before:\n\n![](images/5f/5f2eb6ab39ea67466d7a42a8809b8a039a84f9a20a13cca3659d81f669a772a5.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075046 #edb/ref 1065 8572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075046 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "058743c7-e03e-521c-92ce-c1a5fd00663f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075046 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075052 #edb/ref 1064 "Remember that a *unit* fraction is a fraction containing ![](images/cc/ccee5f5be0445cce59832a3def74f395a7d05cc12dc630fb36baf8f3d2d448bd.svg) in the numerator. So, for example, the fractions \n\n![](images/2e/2e38b15a8f3be74e15b87e81264b66571e593de7250dc902aeaa0e7bc1c506bf.svg)\nare both unit fractions, whereas \n\n![](images/d1/d157ce836a54214740d295f545d4c94d8c57d54d92ac7f075ed9b92556c932b1.svg)\nare *not* unit fractions.\n\nIn this lesson, we will use models to divide unit fractions by whole numbers. \n\nLet's use a fraction model to find the value of \n\n![](images/d8/d8815c6716e962ce4aebee24d004d4c591a1bc35c5fdebcd87d26ace61ead7fb.svg)\nWe start with a square model of ![](images/9f/9fcebe90d169f37bbdbf55f9e4ac86ceaf6ea6d573d101f382a12008bb0c5c2a.svg) split *vertically*.\n\n![](images/cc/ccfe0f457342c456200c98cfc46e63bdc1153698282085164eeafae5c2092c45.png)\n\nTo divide ![](images/9a/9ae11798da63cafee6ce5ccf824a7b96f9f7407a4ee445d1431d907c36f5dd1b.svg) by ![](images/db/dbfe30ca4e672459c180bd90f25f84f025162b8ce73260773fb1fbdf39b0a052.svg) we split the above whole into ![](images/6f/6f33d8df0d08ae9bddce540bedf62f46e93e748e4a5b6fd85b1dc626953e44a6.svg) equal parts *horizontally*. Then, we remove any shaded parts that are not in the bottom row.\n\n![](images/79/7940073ba05d3136ee1d465530a60bc3624b7aa06fd7484d30797758a28976d7.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents ![](images/42/42accbcb8cbfd14800ac4a27bc87d5ba529ff4173a91499f32705cd41b0c88b0.svg) of the whole.\n\nTherefore,\n![](images/f1/f14a0f0f9efb98bdfe6cf40f4b1bbb2c88dc20272caaf9d604fcc44bce79d53c.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075052 #edb/ref 1065 8924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075052 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5efb47fa-47c0-50c8-9c53-0a64bfc732ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075052 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075055 #edb/ref 1064 "We can use fraction models to understand how to multiply fractions.\n\nFor example, let's suppose that we wish to compute the following product of fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3} \\times \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nLet's start by representing $\\dfrac23$ using a square fraction model, like the one below. Notice that we have split the fraction *vertically*.\n\n![](images/dc/dc72f1bc6250e2ca39cf424847d3e592858d8cd6765cade9d357a139bbd906b0.png)\n\nSince we need to multiply the above fraction by $\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}2}$, we split the whole into $\\color{blue}2$ equal parts *horizontally*. This way, each of the original pieces is split in half. Then, we only keep the shaded pieces in the bottom row.\n\n![](images/41/41d47349763b17ed06777b732997ab4f229088b38252c398854a92a1d62ccb6a.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we see that the shaded part represents $\\dfrac{2}{6}$ of the whole.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3} \\times \\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{2}{6}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we can simplify this answer further by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2.$ However, in this lesson, we'll focus just on multiplication and not worry about simplifying the final answers." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075055 #edb/ref 1065 8959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075055 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7d4c2f3b-ffc4-5837-b2b2-961166892aef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075055 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186140356 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186509850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186140356 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186140356 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186140356 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186509850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186140356 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "92b427cb-9398-5368-a038-ebdf307e71ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186142772 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186142772 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186143193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186142772 #edb/ref