2858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193795 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193795 #edb/ref 1061 88305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193795 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1b181827-1135-5056-b586-5b3139e0ce58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193795 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193796 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193796 #edb/ref 1061 88308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193796 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c1d11338-8db9-53d5-b934-67da3916921a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193796 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193797 #edb/ref 1061 88309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193797 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6ab5f02a-0507-5d82-88b7-8426ecc223d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193797 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193931 #edb/ref 1064 "We can use our knowledge of fraction models to multiply fractions by whole numbers. \n\nFor example, let's consider the following product:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{10} \\times 2.\n$$\n\nWe start by drawing a fraction model for $\\dfrac{3}{10}{:}$ \n\n![](images/74/7441921087fa0748dc0de6843c45c93fd9c492bade22fc8ae31192fd9c943016.png)\n\n \n\nMultiplying $\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{10}$ by $\\color{blue}2$ means that we need to repeat the fraction $\\color{blue}2$ times.\n\n![](images/88/88ba96e731a6242aecea0a6b059933e128efdf6bbb52f37a0a73e57a004ab96c.png)\n\n \n\nNow, we count the number of shaded pieces. \n\nThere are ${\\color{red}3} \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 6$ shaded pieces in total. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{10} \\times 2 = \\dfrac{6}{10}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193931 #edb/ref 1065 8518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193931 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bfe48a90-960a-5004-8a63-a58ea72103ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193931 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
